      "inputTokens": 95,
      "outputTokens": 618,
      "costUsd": 0.0056095,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here, with the power used by a home's air conditioner across a hot day. Power is how fast energy is used, measured in kilowatts, and here it peaks at four kilowatts at two in the afternoon. So how much energy does it use, from eight in the morning to eight at night? A quick guess is four kilowatts for twelve hours, which is forty-eight kilowatt hours. Is the true answer more, or less?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i13/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1293,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 1.026000000000053,
        "after": "day.",
        "before": "The"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "hours",
          "start": 17.951999999999988,
          "end": 18.286166666666652
        },
        {
          "text": "adds up",
          "start": 37.243999999999915,
          "end": 37.605999999999916
        },
        {
          "text": "across",
          "start": 42.27799999999997,
          "end": 42.57028571428569
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 43.08,
      "peakFraction": 0.867462158203125,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [
        {
          "word": "power",
          "start": 27.080000000000066,
          "end": 27.381000000000064,
          "score": 0.488
        }
      ],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 155,
      "outputTokens": 1077,
      "costUsd": 0.0097705,
      "pronunciationText": "Less, because the air conditioner only draws four kilowatts at two o'clock. Here's how to get it exactly. Take a thin strip of time, a tiny part of an hour. Over that strip, the power barely changes, so the energy is power times time, in kilowatts times hours, which is kilowatt hours. On the graph, that's the strip's area. Add every strip, make them thinner, and you get the area under the power graph. That's the definite integral of power over the day. The same idea works for any rate. Integrate a rate over an interval, and you get the amount it adds up to. The area's units are the graph's units up, times its units across."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i13/scene-03.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1190,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.9019999999999859,
        "after": "twelve.",
        "before": "The"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "hours",
