      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 139,
      "outputTokens": 1116,
      "costUsd": 0.010113500000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "Units also stop a common mistake. A factory finds that each extra item costs fifty minus zero point two x dollars to make, when it's already making x items. That's a rate, in dollars per item. Raising production from one hundred to two hundred items costs the integral of that rate, from one hundred to two hundred. The primitive is fifty x minus zero point one x squared, and the answer is two thousand dollars. Dollars per item, times items, gives dollars. But integrating a total, like a profit in dollars, gives dollars times items. That isn't the change in profit you were asked for. So integrate a rate, and you get an amount."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-05",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i13/scene-05.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1114,
      "worstWordGap": {
