      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 171,
      "outputTokens": 1201,
      "costUsd": 0.0108945,
      "pronunciationText": "Now a region bounded by the y-axis. A drainage channel has sides shaped like y equals x squared, in metres, with y measured up from the bottom. The water is one metre deep. What area of the cross-section, the shape you'd see if you cut straight across, does the water fill? Put the y-axis down the middle of the channel. Then the right half of the water is bounded by the curve, the y-axis and the water surface. Cut it into thin horizontal strips, like layers of water. At height y, a layer reaches from the y-axis out to x equals root y. So integrate root y with respect to y, from zero to one, which gives two thirds. The channel is even about the y-axis, so double it: four thirds of a square metre."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-07",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i13/scene-07.wav",
